                StoryPart(2, "The Inventory", listOf(
                    "With the caretaker's permission and adult supervision, the club made a list of visible items. They noted dimensions, labels, and apparent condition without climbing on stacks or testing unstable frames. The caretaker identified materials that needed disposal and others that could be assessed by the school's maintenance team.",
                    "Ms Eze explained that painted scenery might contain materials unsuitable for casual sanding or cutting. The students therefore did not attempt repairs. They marked items for professional review and focused on designing a production that could use safe, approved pieces as they were."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Play Without a Palace", listOf(
                    "The new script was set in a travelling library, but the first sketches showed an elaborate palace, a train station, and a forest. Tunde suggested that the club build a single flexible set that could become different places through lighting, signs, and movable props. The idea reduced the amount of construction needed.",
                    "The director liked the concept and asked the cast to test it with simple paper signs. A blue panel became a river, then a library wall; a row of chairs became a waiting room. The students discovered that the audience could imagine a location without a detailed imitation of it."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Frame Check", listOf(
                    "The maintenance team inspected the old frames and approved two for supervised reuse after minor professional repairs. Other pieces were labelled unsafe and scheduled for proper disposal. The club accepted the decision, even though one painted doorway looked perfect for the play.",
                    "A technician explained how the approved frames would be secured and checked before every performance. Students were told not to alter the structure or attach heavy objects. Tunde added the technician's conditions to the rehearsal plan so that creative choices would stay within the approved setup."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "Costumes from the Costume Room", listOf(
                    "The costume cupboard held coats, scarves, and hats from earlier productions. The club checked the sign-out record and asked permission before selecting anything. Some items were too delicate or belonged to another group, so they remained stored. The costume supervisor showed the students how to label and return each piece.",
                    "The cast decided to use a small set of neutral accessories rather than create a different costume for every scene. They wrote down what each actor needed and arranged a fitting session with the supervisor. This made costume changes simpler and reduced the risk of losing borrowed items."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Rehearsal with Changes", listOf(
                    "During rehearsal, the flexible set made scene changes quicker, but one transition confused the audience invited to a preview. The director asked the cast to add a spoken line and a clear sign before changing locations. The students tested the adjustment several times until the sequence felt natural.",
                    "Ms Eze reminded them that feedback was useful when it described what viewers actually experienced. The club did not defend every first idea. They changed what was unclear and kept the parts that helped the story."
